{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"The multi-voter execution landed two results. The declared comparisons returned unknown only through a declaration defect (the result events lacked the snapshots field the applicability conditions referenced), yet the raw events show both the configuration loss and its decision-formation consequence: with RestoreCommittedLogs on, the restarted leader of a five-voter cluster came back at configuration index 1 with three servers against its log's index 14 with five (event 15), and after being isolated with one committed voter it became leader with two votes while the committed configuration requires three and the majority side kept its own leader (event 16: leader_below_committed_quorum true, majority_side_leader true); the flag-off control kept index 14 with five servers and did not win (events 18, 19). The mechanism is the stale three-server list feeding quorumSize() and electSelf, so the node's own vote plus one follower suffices — an election the committed configuration would refuse. Because both checks were pre-declared in this question's discriminator, a successor experiment emits the missing applicability field and declares the configuration check and the election check each with its control for fresh execution; the current receipt is cited as raw observation, the earlier native probe's failure to reproduce the minority leader is retained as a schedule-sensitive negative, and the interpretation stays conditional with the defect asserted. Only the leadership decision is observed — no entry-level contradiction after the minority election is claimed.\"}"}}
